{#
  « Le saviez-vous ? » — the discovery dialog (ARCHITECTURE.md §8.95).

  A modal and not the help panel, deliberately: the panel is opened BY the
  reader and coexists with reading the page, while a tip has to be
  noticed. Two behaviours in one component would be a permanent source of
  regression.

  One card at a time, walked in the browser by
  /assets/js/help-discovery.js. Everything is server-rendered here — the
  script never builds a card from a string — and the only network call is
  the one that closes the dialog.

  The question comes BEFORE the title. That is what separates a tip from
  a table of contents: somebody reads « Comment mettre le prénom de
  chacun dans un e-mail groupé ? » and recognises their own problem,
  where « Publipostage » says nothing to whoever does not already know
  the word.

  No <form> around the body and the footer, and that is worth stating:
  a .modal-dialog-scrollable whose body and footer are wrapped in one
  pushes its footer off a phone's screen, because a <form> with no
  `overflow` never shrinks. There is nothing to submit here — the
  persistence is a fetch — so there is no form at all.
#}
{% embed 'partials/modal.html.twig' with {
    id: 'help-discovery-modal',
    title: 'Le saviez-vous ?',
    static_backdrop: false,
} only %}
    {% block body %}
        {# The position indicator sits at the top of the body rather than
           beside the title: the shared skeleton owns the header, and
           reaching into it would start the drift that partial exists to
           stop. #}
        <p class="text-body-secondary small text-end mb-2" data-discovery-position aria-live="polite"></p>

        {% for card in help_discovery.cards %}
            <div class="{{ loop.first ? '' : 'd-none' }}" data-discovery-card data-discovery-id="{{ card.id }}">
                {% if card.question %}
                    <p class="fs-5 fw-semibold mb-3">{{ card.question }}</p>
                {% endif %}
                {# The answer, framed. The question above is the reader's
                   own words; the two lines below are the help topic that
                   answers it, and a light border is what says so at a
                   glance. Without it the three paragraphs are one wall of
                   text in which the title stops being a title. The frame
                   holds the title AND its summary — they are one thing,
                   and a border around the title alone would separate the
                   two halves of the same answer. #}
                <div class="border rounded p-3 mb-3">
                    <p class="fw-semibold mb-1">{{ card.title }}</p>
                    <p class="text-body-secondary mb-0">{{ card.summary }}</p>
                </div>
                <a href="{{ card.url }}" class="icon-link">
                    <i class="bi bi-book" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    En savoir plus
                </a>
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    {% endblock %}

    {% block footer %}
        <div class="d-flex flex-column gap-2 w-100">
            {# Two buttons rather than one whose label the script rewrites:
               every word a reader sees stays in this file. #}
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary w-100" data-discovery-next>Suivant</button>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary w-100 d-none" data-discovery-done
                    data-bs-dismiss="modal">Terminé</button>
            {% if help_discovery.more %}
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-link w-100 d-none" data-discovery-more>
                    Voir d'autres astuces
                </button>
            {% endif %}
            <div class="d-flex flex-wrap justify-content-center gap-3 small">
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0" data-discovery-snooze>
                    Pas avant une semaine
                </button>
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0 text-body-secondary" data-discovery-never>
                    Ne plus me proposer
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    {% endblock %}
{% endembed %}
